/*
 * Lazy Loading (FetchType.LAZY) vs Eager Loading (FetchType.EAGER)
 *
 * 1. Lazy Loading:
 *    - Category data is fetched only when it is accessed.
 *    - It helps avoid fetching unnecessary data.
 *    - It can improve initial query performance.
 *
 * 2. Eager Loading:
 *    - Category data is fetched eagerly when the Product entity is loaded.
 *    - It fetches data even if the category is not accessed.
 *    - It may cause unnecessary database queries or performance overhead.
 */
 

1. Lazy Loading (FetchType.LAZY)
Interview Definition: Lazy loading is a fetching strategy in JPA where related 
entity data is loaded from the database only when it is accessed, rather than 
being loaded eagerly with the parent entity.

2. Eager Loading (FetchType.EAGER)
Interview Definition: Eager loading is a fetching strategy in JPA
 where related entity data is required to be loaded eagerly when the 
 parent entity is loaded, even if the related data is not explicitly accessed.

Easy way to remember:

LAZY → Load when needed. Simple definition: Lazy loading means related data is loaded from the database only when we need it.

// Department data is loaded
System.out.println(dept.getName());

// Employee data is fetched when accessed
System.out.println(dept.getEmployees());

EAGER → Load eagerly.Eager loading means related data is loaded along with the parent entity, even if we don't use it immediately.
// Department data is loaded
System.out.println(dept.getName());

// Employee data is also fetched eagerly
System.out.println(dept.getEmployees());
Simple explanation
When we fetch the department, JPA also fetches its related employee data eagerly.

Even if we don't call dept.getEmployees(), the employees are still required to be fetched.

When we call dept.getEmployees(), the employee data is already fetched.

Lazy vs Eager
LAZY: Load employee data when needed.

EAGER: Fetch employee data eagerly along with the department.

------------------------------------------------

Lazy loading means the associated data is generally loaded when it is accessed, rather than being loaded immediately with the owning entity.

Eager fetching tells JPA that associated data should be fetched eagerly.
 